# Phase 1: Discover {#phase-1-discover}

**Duration:** Day 1-3 (1 week)

**Goal:** Understand the business question and context before touching data

**Key Output:** Signed project charter

**Rule:** No coding during this phase

---

## Why Discovery Matters

**Common mistake:** Start coding on Day 1 to "make progress"

**Reality:** You can code very quickly in the wrong direction. Spending 2 days on discovery saves 2+ weeks of rework.

**ROI:** 10 hours of discovery saves 40 hours of rework

---

## Discovery Activities

### 1. Pre-Kickoff Research

**Before meeting with your sponsor, get smart on the topic:**

#### Background Research (60-90 min)
- [ ] Search email for previous discussions on this topic
- [ ] Review existing reports or dashboards
- [ ] Read relevant policies, procedures, documentation
- [ ] Check if similar analysis has been done before
- [ ] Look up unfamiliar terms, acronyms, systems

**Ask yourself:**
- What's the business context?
- Why is this needed now?
- Who are the stakeholders?
- What do I not understand yet?

---

#### Draft Questions for Kickoff (30 min)

**Prepare questions in 4 categories:**

**Business Context:**
- What business decision will this analysis inform?
- What's driving the need for this analysis right now?
- What's the timeline and why does it matter?
- Who will use this analysis?

**Current State:**
- How is this currently being tracked (if at all)?
- What existing reports or metrics exist?
- What are the known issues or gaps?
- What's been tried before?

**Success Criteria:**
- What does "done" look like?
- How will we know this project was successful?
- What format should the deliverable be?
- What would make this analysis not useful?

**Scope Boundaries:**
- What's explicitly IN scope?
- What's explicitly OUT of scope?
- Are there any constraints? (time, data, resources, systems)
- If we run out of time, what's the priority?

**Pro tip:** Email these questions to your sponsor before the meeting so they can prepare.

---

### 2. Kickoff Meeting

**Duration:** 60 minutes

**Attendees:** You + Project Sponsor (+ other key stakeholders if appropriate)

**Your job:** Listen more than you talk. Take detailed notes. Ask clarifying questions.

---

#### Meeting Agenda (Share This Beforehand)

**1. Context & Background (10 min)**
- Sponsor explains the situation
- What's happening and why it matters
- History or previous attempts

**2. Business Question & Decision (15 min)**
- What specific question are we answering?
- What decision will be made based on this?
- What happens if we find X? What about Y?
- Is there a hypothesis we're testing?

**3. Deliverable Format & Audience (15 min)**
- Who is the primary audience?
- What format works best? (deck, dashboard, Excel, report)
- Can you show me an example of "good"?
- Is this one-time or recurring?
- What level of detail is appropriate?

**4. Scope & Timeline (10 min)**
- What must be included?
- What should explicitly NOT be included?
- What's the deadline and why?
- What's the minimum viable version?

**5. Next Steps & Open Questions (10 min)**
- What do I need access to?
- Who else should I talk to?
- What blockers do you anticipate?
- When should we check in next?

---

#### Critical Questions to Ask

**On Business Impact:**
> "If this analysis shows [X], what would you do differently?"
>
> "What's the cost of not having this analysis?"

**On Deliverable:**
> "Can you show me what 'good' looks like? Any examples?"
>
> "What's more important: speed or comprehensiveness?"

**On Scope:**
> "If we had to cut something, what would it be?"
>
> "What are we explicitly NOT analyzing?"

**On Success:**
> "How will you use this analysis?"
>
> "What would disappoint you about the final product?"

**Take verbatim notes** when they describe what they want. Read it back to confirm understanding.

